Qwen3.5 CUDA INT8 prefill: exact tensor-core projections. Gates first, then results and decision. The scripts (gemm_probe.rs, paired.sh) stay on the maintainer's machine.

=== GATES (written before any kernel change) ===
Qwen3.5 CUDA INT8 prefill: question, gates and stopping conditions (written before any kernel change)

Finding from reading the code, before measuring: Qwen3.5 calls matmul_precise -> matmul_quant_precise, which never uses the tensor-core kernel
(matmul_q8_mma, used by Qwen3) and runs the warp-per-row f32 FMA kernel (matmul_q8, 8 tokens per tile). The tensor-core kernel splits f32
activations into two bf16 parts (~16 bits), which is not what the strict hybrid HF gates were built on.
Hypothesis: the projections are limited by that scalar kernel (layout/instruction bound), not by memory or the power cap; a tensor-core kernel
whose activation split is exact (three bf16 parts = 24 bits, int8 codes are exact in bf16, f32 accumulation) keeps f32-grade results.

Shapes (Qwen3.5-4B, hidden 2560, mlp 9216, 32 layers): gate/up 9216x2560, down 2560x9216, DeltaNet qkv 8192x2560, z 4096x2560, out 2560x4096,
full attention q 8192x2560, k/v 1024x2560, o 2560x4096. Prompts of 128, 512, 1024, 2048 tokens.

Step 1 (profile): one-GEMM timing of the current precise kernel vs the existing hi/lo tensor-core kernel on those shapes at 128..2048 tokens;
  achieved TFLOP/s and GB/s against the card (probe: 262 GB/s sustained, 11 fp32 / 27.8 bf16-tensor TFLOP/s). Decides: scalar-kernel-bound or not.
Candidate: ONE change, an exact three-part activation split in the existing mma.cu path, selected by the precise Int8 call for >= 16 tokens.
  Same weights and layout, no new buffers (shared memory only), decode and 1..15-token passes untouched.

Gates (all must hold, else NO-GO and the tree is restored):
  C1 one GEMM: relative RMS and normalized max error against an f64 product no worse than 2x the current precise kernel's on the same inputs.
  C2 pinned HF verify (Qwen3.5-4B Q8 CUDA, 128-step reference): PASS with unchanged strict gates (normalized max <= 1e-4, relative RMS <= 1e-5, exact greedy ids).
  C3 greedy text of fresh chat and code tasks identical to the accepted build (paired, same session); hybrid-state, checkpoint, batching,
     cancellation and rollback tests pass (full CUDA and scalar workspace suites at the end).
  P1 kernel level: the MLP and QKV projections at 512 tokens at least 2x faster.
  P2 end to end: prefill tok/s at 512, 1024 and 2048 at least 1.5x (i.e. +50%) in interleaved old/new runs; TTFT of a matched chat and a code task
     at least 30% lower; decode tok/s within +-3%.
  M1 process VRAM peak not higher by more than 64 MiB at any prompt length; RSS within 5%.
  Power is read with --sample-gpu and reported; no efficiency claim without it.
Stop: at most 3 kernel iterations. If the end-to-end gain is under 20% after the second iteration, or any C gate fails, stop NO-GO.
No tuning sweep beyond the shapes above; no change to Granite, Qwen3, BF16 paths, decode kernels, loaders, scheduler or APIs.

=== ROOT CAUSE ===
Qwen3.5 calls matmul_precise -> matmul_quant_precise, which always took the warp-per-row f32 FMA kernel (matmul_q8, 8 tokens per tile). The
tensor-core kernel (matmul_q8_mma, used by Qwen3) was never reached. One-GEMM timing, Int8 group 128, Qwen3.5-4B shapes, RTX 5050 at 40 W:
  shape (rows x cols)      tokens   f32 kernel (accepted)      tensor core, 2-part split      exact 3-part (candidate)
  mlp gate/up 9216x2560     512      18.9 ms  1.28 TF/s         3.0 ms  7.95 TF/s              3.8-4.4 ms  5.5-6.4 TF/s
  mlp down    2560x9216     512      19.9 ms  1.22 TF/s         4.3 ms  5.61 TF/s              4.3-4.7 ms  5.1-5.6 TF/s
  delta qkv   8192x2560     512      20.5 ms  1.05 TF/s         3.1 ms  6.95 TF/s              3.7-4.6 ms  4.7-5.9 TF/s
  out proj    2560x4096     512       8.7 ms  1.23 TF/s         1.4 ms  7.49 TF/s              1.9 ms     5.7 TF/s
  The accepted kernel reaches 1.0-1.4 TFLOP/s, 11% of the card's 11 TFLOP/s fp32 peak and 4% of its 27.8 bf16 tensor peak; the cost is the
  kernel (one weight row per warp, no operand reuse across tokens), not weight reads (the MLP moves 23.6 MB per GEMM at 512 tokens) or the power cap.
  Error against an f64 product (relative RMS): f32 kernel 1.0e-7..1.6e-7; 2-part tensor core 2.5e-6 (about 20x worse, why Qwen3.5 avoided it);
  3-part split, accumulation inside the tensor core: 4.0e-7 (iteration 1, failed gate C1: 4x); 3-part split with each k-step summed in a fresh
  accumulator and added in f32 (iteration 2): 1.3e-7..2.0e-7 (passes C1). Two iterations used of three allowed.

=== CHANGE ===
mma.cu: matmul_mma<FORMAT, SPLITS>; SPLITS = 3 splits each f32 activation into bf16 high, middle and low parts (24 bits, exact), three
multiply-accumulates per tile, each k-step's result added to the group partial sum in f32. New entry matmul_q8_mma_exact. quant.rs:
matmul_quant_precise for Int8 and 16 or more tokens selects it; Int4, 1..15 tokens, 2..4-token batched decode and BF16 are unchanged.
No new buffers (shared memory 3 KB more per block), same weights and layout, no new API.

